---
# yamllint disable rule:line-length
# Recognises a `.filter(p)` whose predicate is not built at the call site but
# LOADED — from a method parameter, a local, or a static field (#1014, the
# puzzle `1008-411ebd40` left on `215-recognize-named-filter`). The `refArg` case
# of `streams/closures.yml` is the shape:
#
#     static int viaRefArg(Predicate<Integer> p, Function<Integer, Integer> f) {
#       return Stream.of(1, 2, 3, 4, 5).filter(p).map(f)
#         .mapToInt(Integer::intValue).sum();
#     }
#
# THE ANCHOR IS THE CALL, not the operand. `215-recognize-named-filter` keys on
# the `new`/`dup`/`<init>` run in front of the stream call, which says both that
# an object is being built and where the run begins. There is no such run here —
# the operand is one `aload` or one `getstatic`, and nothing about that opcode on
# its own distinguishes the predicate from the receiver Stream. So this rule keys on
# the stream call's own descriptor, `(Ljava/util/function/Predicate;)Ljava/util/stream/Stream;`,
# which names the functional interface, and takes the opcode IMMEDIATELY in front
# of it. That opcode is the predicate and cannot be anything else: a JVM call site
# pushes the receiver first and the argument last, so whatever is adjacent to a
# one-argument `Stream.filter` is what it consumes.
#
# Adjacency is also the whole of the guard. Where javac puts a label or a frame
# between the load and the call, the pattern simply does not match and the
# operation stays native — a missed fusion, never a wrong one. And a `filter`
# whose argument is an expression rather than a load ends in some other opcode:
# `invokespecial` for a `new Foo()`, which `215-recognize-named-filter` takes,
# `invokedynamic` for a lambda or a methodref, which `111` lifted long before
# the 2xx phase, `invokestatic` for `filter(makeIt())`, which nothing takes yet.
#
# Only `aload` and `getstatic` are admitted, and the reason is `502`. What
# `317` builds out of this pragma is a `state` block that the fused method's
# caller runs once, and `502` relocates that block into the very method the
# opcode came from — so the opcode has to be one that pushes the operator ON ITS
# OWN. `aload` and `getstatic` are; `getfield` is not, because its receiver was
# pushed by an opcode ahead of the run and would be left behind, stranded on the
# caller's stack. An instance field therefore stays native.
#
# Everything downstream is shared with the constructed shape. The pragma is the
# same `Φ.hone.named-filter` with the same four erased type fields — `Predicate.test`
# tells us nothing about the element type, exactly as it tells `215` nothing —
# and the only difference is what its `make` block holds: one load opcode here,
# a whole `new`/`dup`/`args`/`<init>` run there. `317` splices whichever it is
# between the `dup` and the `List.add` of the append, and `247` leaves a filter alone, since a predicate
# hands its item on untouched.
name: recognize-ref-filter
pattern: |
  ⟦
    φ ↦ Φ.jeo.class,
    𝐵-class-head-before,
    name ↦ 𝑒-class-name,
    𝐵-class-head-after,
    𝜏-caller ↦ ⟦
      φ ↦ Φ.jeo.method,
      𝐵-caller-head,
      body ↦ ⟦
        𝐵-body-head,
        𝜏-load ↦ ⟦
          φ ↦ Φ.jeo.opcode.𝜏-load-op,
          𝐵-load-operands
        ⟧,
        𝜏-invokeinterface ↦ ⟦
          φ ↦ Φ.jeo.opcode.invokeinterface,
          𝜏6 ↦ "java/util/stream/Stream",
          𝜏7 ↦ "filter",
          𝜏8 ↦ "(Ljava/util/function/Predicate;)Ljava/util/stream/Stream;",
          𝜏9 ↦ Φ.true
        ⟧,
        𝐵-body-tail
      ⟧,
      𝐵-caller-tail
    ⟧,
    𝐵-class-tail
  ⟧
result: |
  ⟦
    φ ↦ Φ.jeo.class,
    𝐵-class-head-before,
    name ↦ 𝑒-class-name,
    𝐵-class-head-after,
    𝜏-caller ↦ ⟦
      φ ↦ Φ.jeo.method,
      𝐵-caller-head,
      body ↦ ⟦
        𝐵-body-head,
        𝜏-named ↦ ⟦
          φ ↦ Φ.hone.named-filter,
          class ↦ 𝑒-class-name,
          make ↦ ⟦
            𝜏-make-load ↦ ⟦
              φ ↦ Φ.jeo.opcode.𝜏-load-op,
              𝐵-load-operands
            ⟧
          ⟧,
          bridge-input ↦ "Ljava/lang/Object;",
          bridge-output ↦ "Ljava/lang/Object;",
          accepted ↦ "Ljava/lang/Object;",
          returned ↦ "Ljava/lang/Object;",
          static ↦ "true"
        ⟧,
        𝐵-body-tail
      ⟧,
      𝐵-caller-tail
    ⟧,
    𝐵-class-tail
  ⟧
when:
  or:
    - eq: [𝜏-load-op, 'aload']
    - eq: [𝜏-load-op, 'getstatic']
where:
  - meta: 𝜏-named
    function: random-tau
  - meta: 𝜏-make-load
    function: random-tau
